      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a15.5.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g15.5.4', 'g15.5.9', 'g15.5.22'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“I am the vine; you are the branches”",
          body: "The last of the seven “I am” sayings, and the tenderest. In the Old Testament, Israel was God’s vine — planted, tended, and (all too often) fruitless and wild (Isaiah 5; Psalm 80). Jesus says he is the *true* vine, the real Israel, the one in whom the story of a fruitful people finally comes good — and his people are branches *in him.* The word that beats through the passage like a pulse is **abide** (*menō*, “remain, stay, dwell”) — ten times in ten verses. The Christian life is not first a program of effort but a *staying*: keeping the connection open, remaining in living union with Christ the way a branch simply *rests* in the vine and lets the sap rise. And from that one image, three truths press in. First, **fruit is the aim** — a branch exists to bear, and the Father, the vinedresser, both *prunes* the fruitful (his knife is not punishment but care, cutting back what drains life so more may grow) and removes the dead. Second, **fruit is his, not ours** — the branch produces nothing by straining; it bears simply by *not letting go.* Third, and most bracing: **“apart from me you can do nothing.”** Not *little* — *nothing;* nothing of eternal weight, no fruit that lasts. Severed from the vine a branch does not become an independent plant; it becomes firewood (v. 6). The whole secret of a life that counts is hidden in this quiet command: *stay close.* Abide, and the fruit will come, because it was never yours to manufacture — only his to grow through a branch that remains.",
        },
      ],
